The finding holds and "major" is the right severity. data/capabilities.json:2836 does define CNT.COOK.ddc as "Derived-data cache keyed by source path & file modification time". That wording contradicts four other places in the framework data:
- the owner's purpose, "derived-data cache and content-addressed store" (skills.json:2530);
- the shared-ddc registry limit, "content-addressed/verified" (untrusted-inputs.json:290);
- C-COOK "cache keys, determinism class";
- CNT.COOK.incremental-equivalence ("Incremental cook equals clean cook").

This is material. PRF.METH.pipeline-budgets explicitly budgets "DDC hit rate" and "cook throughput". Autonomous agents implementing the capability as written would build a machine-local mtime cache. That cache would miss on every sync and every farm machine, so CNT.COOK.shared-cache ("Team/cloud derived-data cache") could not work. It would also serve stale hits when the processor version or settings change, which breaks incremental-equivalence. That is a core pipeline correctness and performance defect, not a matter of style.

The finding is within K-PERF's mandate because it is about pipeline budgets and hit rate. Neither docs/06 nor docs/00 records a scope decision that favours mtime keys. The proposed change is technically sound. A content-addressed key over source content, transitive input hashes, processor version, settings and platform variant is the practice used by Bazel/Buck, UE DDC and Unity ADBv2/Accelerator. The other two proposals are also sound: a C-COOK obligation that processors declare every key input, and a legacy pattern for timestamp/path-keyed caches.

K-TOOLS, K-LEGACY, K-ARCH and K-SEC report the same finding independently. That duplication does not make it any less valid. The one small improvement: the new wording could allow mtime as a local change-detection hint only, as K-TOOLS proposes.
